    "lawCode": "Add §344-e, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7428\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to designating a portion\nof the state highway system as the \"Lieutenant Michael M. McGreevy,\nJr.  Memorial Bridge\"\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to designate the bridge on State Route\n417, over Dodge Creek, in the town of Portville, county of\nCattaraugus, as the \"Lieutenant Michael M. McGreevy, Jr. Memorial\nBridge\"\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends the highway law by adding a new section 344-e, which\ndesignates the bridge on State Route 417, crossing over Dodge Creek,\nin the town of Portville, county of Cattaraugus, as the \"Lieutenant\nMichael M. McGreevy, Jr. Memorial Bridge\"\n\nSection 2: States that the commissioner of transportation shall\nprovide for the installation and maintenance of adequate signing of\nthe state highway systems as designated pursuant to section 1 of this\nact. The designation will be for ceremonial purposes only, and the\nbridge's official name will not be changed as a result of this act.\n\nSection 3: Sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nLieutenant Michael M. McGreevy resided in Portville since the age of 9\nand went on to attend the United States Naval Academy in Annapolis,\nMaryland to pursue a stellar career in the U.S. Navy. After graduating\nfrom the Naval Academy, Lt. McGreevy began his career as a Surface\nWarfare Officer, and soon enrolled in Basic Underwater Demolition/SEAL\n(BUD/S) training to become a Navy SEAL, and graduated as Honor Man of\nhis BUD/S class in 2000. After serving in Africa as a part of SEAL\nTeam 10, he deployed to Afghanistan in April of 2005.\n\nLt. McGreevy was part of a dedicated Naval Special Warfare team\nfighting the Taliban, a fundamentalist regime that a U.S.-led\ncoalition knocked from power in Afghanistan in 2001, and continued to\nconduct guerilla operations, particularly along the Pakistan border.\nLt. McGreevy worked to help ensure al Qaeda terrorists could not train\nin, nor launch strikes from Afghanistan since their lethal attack on\nthe World Trade Center in New York on September 11, 2001.\n\nOn June 28, 2005, Lt. McGreevy, seven of his fellow Navy SEALS, eight\nArmy NIGHT STALKER commandos, and the helicopter's crew were killed in\naction when their helicopter took significant enemy fire and crashed\nin the mountains of Afghanistan's Kunar Province while attempting a\ndangerous nighttime operation to reinforce and rescue a four man SEAL\nteam that had been ambushed by Taliban forces.\n\nFor his service, Lt. McGreevy was awarded the Bronze Star with Combat\n\"V\" for Valor, the Purple Heart, the Combat Action Ribbon, and the\nAfghanistan Campaign Medal, posthumously. A highly decorated veteran,\nLt. McGreevy was also awarded the Navy and Marine Corps Commendation\nMedal, the Navy and Marine Corps Achievement Medal (3), the Navy\nMeritorious Unit Commendation Medal, the National Defense Service\nMedal (2), the Armed Forces Expeditionary Medal, the Global War on\nTerrorism Expeditionary Medal, the Global War on Terrorism Service\nMedal, and the Sea Service Deployment Ribbon (2).\n\nLt. Michael M. McGreevy served his nation at the highest levels. We\nowe him, as we owe all of our veterans, a tremendous debt of gratitude\nfor their service and sacrifice. Lt. McGreevy made the ultimate\nsacrifice in a brave attempt to bring his fellow brothers-in-arms home\nsafely. Cattaraugus County has expressed their strong support for\nmemorializing this bridge in his honor. It will express, in\nperpetuity, gratitude and a fitting tribute for his ultimate\nsacrifice.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
